The check uses the repo's evaluate.py tolerances (0.005; 0.015 for the set size) on seven metrics, among them the share of photo families stopped by the re-aim gate: that one averages over 87 families, so a single borderline crop in a small family moves it by up to ~0.006. Different runtimes round differently and flip a handful of crops that sit exactly on a threshold (see 'same arch-B answer').

Per-class recall (top-1): PyTorch FP32 (GPU): corrosion 0.622, crack 0.761, dent 0.852, missing-head 0.951, paint-peel-off 0.717, scratch 0.530, no-defect 0.911

PyTorch FP32 vs the dashboard's Model page / repo evaluate.py (0.8036 / 0.7514): MATCH (0.8036 / 0.7514).

Whole benchmark run: 823 s. One run, one seed; the RTX 2050 is not the Jetson Orin NX and this laptop CPU is not a Raspberry Pi (relative speed-ups carry over, absolute ms do not).

